task: dataflow-commit-window
agent: opus-dataflow
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/dataflow-commit-window
worktree: ../okay-wt-dcw1
spec: specs/dataflow.md, stage 9

why: stage 8 left one window open and named it. A pane is WRITTEN
  while its epoch is being absorbed and the epoch is COMMITTED after,
  so a coordinator that dies in between has written panes the journal
  does not know about, and its successor writes them again. For a
  keyed writer that is harmless — same key, same value, one row — and
  the backlog entry I wrote said the ways to close it were worse than
  it is. That was true of the two roads I had thought of, and both
  were the wrong shape.

what: the engine cannot close this window alone, and it should stop
  pretending the question is whether it can. What it CAN do is hand
  the writer the two moments that let the writer close it:

    Sink.committed(epoch)  — the journal now holds this epoch
    Sink.recovered(epoch)  — resuming; anything after this is not real

  Defaults are no-ops, so nothing existing changes and nothing unused
  costs anything. `and` forwards to both halves. `Cluster.stream`
  calls the first right after `journal.save` and the second once on
  resume; a batch run is one epoch and calls the first at the end.

  A writer that STAGES — accumulates an epoch's panes and moves them
  into the store on `committed`, drops what `recovered` says never
  happened — is then exactly-once across a coordinator death, in
  OFFERS and not merely in outcome. That is Flink's two-phase-commit
  sink, and it is twenty lines here rather than a framework, for the
  same reason the checkpoint was a `save` call: the epoch loop is
  lock-step.

what this is NOT: a promise that the ENGINE is exactly-once at the
  sink. A writer that cannot stage still gets at-least-once with an
  identity, which is stage 6c's answer and remains the default. What
  changes is that the honest ceiling is now reachable by a writer
  that can, instead of being out of reach for everyone.

predict: the mechanism is small; the risk is the test being weaker
  than it looks. A staging writer that is offered each pane once
  proves nothing unless the death is INSIDE the window it exists for
  — so the test must assert that the unstaged writer, on the same
  schedule, IS offered a pane twice.
